<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pál Prónay</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1874">1874</a>–c. 1947</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pál Prónay was a Hungarian army officer who commanded one of the paramilitary detachments of the White Terror after the fall of the Hungarian Soviet Republic in 1919. Opposing the transfer of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, his irregulars proclaimed the short-lived Banate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leitha">Leitha</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oberwart">Oberwart</a> in October 1921. Arrested by Soviet forces in 1945, he died in captivity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pal-pronay.jpg" alt="Pál Prónay" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: József KOSSAK (1855-1922) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PalPronay.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
